{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pl-09-mixtures-adulteration-blended-cost",
  "version": 1,
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Mixtures and Adulteration for Profit",
    "description": "Find a blend’s true cost and sale margin when goods of different values or purities are combined.",
    "teachingApproach": "Develop the commercial relationship step by step, explain why each rate uses its base, show intermediate amounts, check signs and units, then practice transfer with misconception-specific retries.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 50,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 12 “Profit and Loss,” printed pages 374–425. Approved scope: Calculate weighted cost per unit for mixtures of goods with different purchase prices, then determine a selling rate or profit. Solve blend ratios when a target mean cost or sale price is given, using weighted equations or alligation with its assumptions explained. Include an adulterant with lower cost and distinguish composition from profit calculation. Use consistent mass/volume units and check that a blend cost lies between component costs when both quantities are positive. Ground coverage in solved examples 16–17 and objective mixture questions."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Mixtures and Adulteration for Profit: Find a blend’s true cost and sale margin when goods of different values or purities are combined. We establish the model, explain each step, and practice with targeted feedback.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A mixture has a total cost equal to the sum of the costs of its components. Its cost per unit is total cost divided by total quantity; it is not the plain average of component prices unless quantities match.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_2": {
      "id": "teach_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Example: mix 80 kg at ₹13.50/kg with 120 kg at ₹16/kg. Total cost=80×13.5+120×16=₹3,000; total mass=200 kg; blended cost=₹15/kg.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_3": {
      "id": "teach_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "To earn 16% on that blend, sale rate=1.16×15=₹17.40/kg. Compute the weighted cost first, then apply the margin to the blend’s CP.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_4"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_4": {
      "id": "teach_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When component prices and a target mean price are known, alligation compares their distances from the mean. Quantity ratio of cheaper to dearer material is (dearer−mean):(mean−cheaper), under a two-component linear blend.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_5"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_5": {
      "id": "teach_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For oil costing ₹100/kg and an adulterant costing ₹50/kg, a target blend cost ₹80/kg gives oil:adulterant=(80−50):(100−80)=30:20=3:2.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_6"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_6": {
      "id": "teach_6",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Alligation is a shortcut for weighted average, not a separate profit law. Verify it by substituting quantities and calculating the mean directly.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_7"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_7": {
      "id": "teach_7",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Adulteration problems may state a resale price and target profit. First infer the average CP per unit from SP and margin, then solve the mixture ratio.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_8"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_8": {
      "id": "teach_8",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Keep components distinct from added profit. A blend costing ₹80/kg sold for ₹96/kg has 20% gain because 16/80=20%; the ₹96 is not the cost mean.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_9"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_9": {
      "id": "teach_9",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If batches have different quantities, weight prices by quantity. Two lots at ₹40/kg and ₹60/kg in a 1:3 mass ratio have average cost ₹55/kg.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_10"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_10": {
      "id": "teach_10",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A concentration statement such as pure ghee mixed with cheaper oil may use volume or mass. Do not treat liters and kilograms as interchangeable unless the problem gives a conversion.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_11"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_11": {
      "id": "teach_11",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A mixture can be sold at a rate below one component’s purchase price and still earn profit if the weighted average CP is lower.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_12"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_12": {
      "id": "teach_12",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If a ratio refers to component quantities, define one ratio unit and scale both components. Total cost and total quantity must use the same scale.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_13"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_13": {
      "id": "teach_13",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A quick bound check: for a blend of two positive quantities, average CP must lie between the two component costs. A result outside that interval signals an arithmetic or weighting error.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_14"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_14": {
      "id": "teach_14",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When selling a blend by the kilogram, calculate SP per kilogram from the full lot. If total lot SP is given, divide by total lot mass before comparing rates.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_15"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_15": {
      "id": "teach_15",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Conclude by distinguishing the blend ratio, average CP, selling rate, profit amount, and profit percent. A problem may ask for any one of these.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "focus_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "focus_1": {
      "id": "focus_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Name the cost, receipt, and percentage base. Choose an operation that follows the transaction wording.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
